const EDU_TIPS=[{t:"Go beyond enslavement. Set the context first, for example African societies before slavery, and teach achievements and contributions as well as persecution. This matters for Black pupils' wellbeing.",q:"It is important that learners understand that there is much more to Black History",src:[SRCU("Child enslavement: second level teaching resource (Education Scotland)","https://education.gov.scot/media/5qwhbbkv/nih350-child-enslavement-2nd-ievel-teaching-resource.pdf","")]},{t:"Centre agency and positive role models. Choose significant individuals who counter victim-centred narratives.",q:"Actively use examples and narratives countering this dominance.",src:[SRCU("Wangari Maathai as a significant individual (Historical Association)","https://www.history.org.uk/primary/categories/687/resource/10445/wangari-maathai-as-a-significant-individual","")]},{t:"Use local history. Local museum and archive collections root Black history in pupils' own streets and communities.",q:"Our learning resources are inspired by our collections and tell the diverse histories and story of Hackney.",src:[SRCU("Hackney Museum learning resources","https://hackney-museum.hackney.gov.uk/schools/learning-resources/","")]},{t:"Plan for emotional impact. Preview difficult material, set a wellbeing action plan for before, during and after, and make sure adults are on hand.",q:"creating your own action plan tailored to your group",src:[SRCU("London, Sugar & Slavery gallery visit resources (London Museum Docklands)","https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/schools-communities/schools/resources/london-sugar-slavery-gallery-visit-resources-for-schools","")]},{t:"Prepare vocabulary and review sources first. Discuss terms such as 'colour bar' and 'discrimination' beforehand, and check documents for language that is no longer acceptable.",q:"The National Archives has created some resources for you to use in your classroom with our video.",src:[SRCU("The Empire Windrush (The National Archives)","https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/the-empire-windrush/","")]},{t:"Let real voices lead. Pair oral testimony with documents, play clips twice and let pupils raise their own questions.",q:"Nothing was a bad idea or spoon-fed to them",src:[SRCU("Engaging children with testimonies of Caribbean migration (British Library)","https://www.bl.uk/stories/blogs/posts/engaging-children-with-testimonies-of-caribbean-migration-part-1","")]},{t:"Teach Black history all year, not only in October. Build it into curriculum planning.",q:"celebrated and integrated into our classrooms all year long",src:[SRCU("Empowering teachers for Black History Month (Chartered College of Teaching)","https://chartered.college/news-blogs/empowering-teachers-for-black-history-month-resources-to-enhance-your-curriculum/","")]},{t:"Start early with mirrors and windows. Even in the early years, books can reflect children's own lives and open views onto others.",q:"readers often seek their mirrors in books.",src:[SRCU("Picturebooks part 6: mirrors and windows (Nursery World)","https://www.nurseryworld.co.uk/articles/picturebooks-part-6-mirrors-and-windows","")]},{t:"Make it whole-school. Pair content with staff racial literacy and anti-racist practice, not one-off lessons.",q:"The framework is designed to enable a whole school/ college approach to racial literacy.",src:[SRCU("Anti-racism charter (National Education Union)","https://neu.org.uk/latest/library/anti-racism-charter","")]}];
